10:47:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Because dyes can still come out of coloured clothes in a mixed wash — it’s just usually much harder to notice. White fabric has no dye of its own, so even a tiny amount of red dye released into the water can settle on it and make it visibly pink. Red dyes in particular are often strong and prone to bleeding, especially from new items, in warm water, or with harsh detergent. In a load of colours: - Some dye may still transfer, but it is masked by the existing colours. - Dark colours can absorb a little dye without looking noticeably different. - Similar shades may not show any change at all. - Many modern coloured garments are “colourfast,” meaning their dye is designed not to wash out much after the first few washes. So colours *can* mix or dull each other over time; whites just reveal the problem immediately. Washing new reds and dark items separately at first, using cool water, and using colour-catcher sheets can help.
