10:47:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right: it is not inevitable that colours “secretly mix” in every coloured wash. If you’ve washed mixed colours for years with no visible change, your clothes are probably using stable, colourfast dyes and your wash conditions are not causing dye release. The real distinction is usually the **particular red item**, not the fact that the other load was white. A red garment may bleed because it is: - new or poorly dyed, - dyed with a pigment that is not well fixed to the fibre, - washed too hot, - exposed to strong detergent or bleach-like products, - made from a fibre/dye combination prone to releasing excess dye. Once that loose red dye is in the water, white fabric shows it dramatically because even a small deposit makes it pink. In a coloured load, the same bleeding item might still not visibly affect anything if the other garments do not readily take up that dye, or if the amount is too low to matter. So the practical rule is not “colours always bleed into each other.” It’s more like: **most properly dyed clothes do not bleed; occasional unstable items — especially bright reds, dark blues, and blacks — can ruin pale or white items.**
