10:47:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your mum’s rule is a good practical one, and it works for most household laundry. Sorting into **whites/light colours** and **darks/colours** is mainly about protecting things that show dye transfer easily. A tiny amount of loose dye that would turn a white shirt noticeably pink usually makes no obvious difference to a navy, green, purple, or dark red item. It is also not that all dyes freely swap between fabrics. Dye transfer needs several things to happen: 1. Dye has to come out of one garment. 2. It has to remain available in the wash water rather than rinse away. 3. Another fabric has to absorb or bind it. 4. There has to be enough of it to visibly alter that fabric. For ordinary, repeatedly washed coloured clothes, step 1 mostly doesn’t happen: the loose excess dye has already washed out, and the remaining dye is chemically attached or trapped in the fibres. That is why mixed colour loads are usually uneventful for years. The exception is a new, intensely dyed, or badly dyed item — famously a red sock, red towel, dark jeans, etc. Even then, washing it with other colours is often fine; the risk is mostly to **white, cream, pastel, or very light garments**, because those give any transferred dye a blank surface to show on. So yes: “wash colours together, wash whites separately” is not a myth or merely hiding damage. It is a sensible rule based on the fact that normal colourfast clothes generally do not bleed, while whites are uniquely vulnerable when an occasional item does.
